    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This paper investigates the effects of COVID-19 stay-at-home orders on new case and death numbers by county in the United States. Although a large body of past literature describes the pandemic’s effect on financial and physical health, there is a gap in our understanding of how government policies influenced individual COVID-19 outcomes. This paper utilizes a multiple fixed-effects regression approach as introduced by Correia (2016) to predict the average treatment effect of these government mandates on new COVID-19 cases and deaths by week. Ultimately, we conclude that the COVID-19 stay-at-home orders disproportionately aided high-income and predominantly White communities, while case and death rates rose after these mandates for low-income and predominantly Black communities. This paper largely supports the understanding that financial and human crises affect vulnerable communities the most, and it raises important policy implications for government aid during these crises.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
